cellent","best","best","best","best","good","best","excellent","best","best","best","best","best","best","best","inaccuracy","great","excellent","good","best","good","best","excellent","best","good","best","best","inaccuracy","mistake","inaccuracy","excellent","inaccuracy","blunder","excellent","best","best","best","excellent","best","best","best","best","good","miss","best","best","best","best","excellent","best","best","good","best","inaccuracy","best","best","blunder","brilliant","best","great","inaccuracy","best","best","excellent","blunder","best","best","best"],"moments":[{"ply":46,"name":"23…Nd7","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"23…Nd7! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Adds a decisive attacker on the e5 pawn: 2 attackers against 1 defender.","Moves the attacked knight to safety.","The next best move, 23…c4+, would drop Black's winning chances from 59% to 40% (+1.12).","23…Nd7 gets the knight out of the way of exf6 and threatens …Nxe5+, winning a pawn.","White was threatening exf6, winning a knight. The knight steps away.","It threatens …Nxe5+, winning a pawn, so White has to spend the next move on it."]},{"ply":58,"name":"29…Rf8","cls":"mistake","color":"b","headline":"29…Rf8 is a mistake: Black's winning chances go from 51% to 38%.","points":["White answers 30.Be5: the bishop aims at the g7 pawn, a weak point in front of the Black king.","Better was 29…Nxf4 (−0.09), which takes the bishop on f4."],"better":"29…Nxf4 threatens …Nxe2, winning a knight. After 30.Rxd8 Rxd8 31.Nxf4 Rd4+ 32.Kf3 Bd2, the rooks are traded (−0.09)."},{"ply":62,"name":"31…c4","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"31…c4 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 53% to 31%.","points":["White answers 32.Rxd8, which takes the rook on d8.","Better was 31…Bd2 (−0.30): the bishop joins the attack: it now hits 2 squares around the White king."],"better":"31…Bd2 joins the attack on the king and adds pressure on the knight on f4. Engine line: 32.Ne3 Rd4+ 33.Kf3 Rfd8 34.Nc2 R4d6 (−0.30)."},{"ply":73,"name":"37.Nxg6","cls":"miss","color":"w","headline":"37.Nxg6 lets the win slip: 37.hxg6 kept White winning. White's winning chances go from 70% to 51%.","points":["Black answers 37…Bf6.","Better was 37.hxg6 (+2.36), which takes the knight on g6."],"better":"37.hxg6 wins a knight. After 37…Rd4+ 38.Kf5 Kf8 39.Rc1 Bb2 40.Rc2, White comes out a piece up (+2.36)."},{"ply":86,"name":"43…Rc8","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"43…Rc8 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 50% to 9%.","points":["White answers 44.Rxb4, which takes the bishop on b4.","Better was 43…Rd2 (0.00)."],"better":"43…Rd2 prepares …Rd1 and …Re1+. Engine line: 44.Nf4 Rd1 45.Ke4 Re1+ 46.Kd5 Rd1+ (0.00)."},{"ply":87,"name":"44.Rxb4","cls":"brilliant","color":"w","headline":"44.Rxb4!! A real sacrifice: White gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Puts the rook on b4 where it can be taken.","Black can take, but after 44…axb4 45.Ne7+ Kh7 46.Nxc8 the engine rates it +6.58.","Takes the bishop on b4.","44.Rxb4 takes the bishop on b4.","Black answers 44…axb4, winning the exchange.","After 44…axb4 45.Ne7+ Kh7 46.Nxc8 b3 47.e7, White comes out a piece up (+6.58)."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"44.Rxb4!! offers the rook on b4. Taking it lets White's pawn become a queen."},{"title":"What White gives up","text":"44.Rxb4 puts the rook on b4 where it can be taken by the a5 pawn. That's a whole rook on offer."},{"title":"What it does instead","text":"It takes the bishop on b4. Black has no bishop left to guard the dark squares around the king (f8, h8)."},{"title":"If Black takes the rook","text":"44…axb4 is met by 45.Ne7+. Then 46.Nxc8 takes the rook on c8, and 47.e7 threatens e8=Q, winning a pawn. White ends up a piece ahead: White is winning (+3.80). This is Black's best try."},{"title":"Without the sacrifice","text":"The best move without the sacrifice, 44.Rxa5, is clearly weaker (+1.27 against +6.58). Black answers 44…Bxa5, taking the rook on a5. That would leave White a little better."}]},{"ply":89,"name":"45.Ne7+","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"45.Ne7+! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["The knight forks the king and the rook: both can't be saved at once.","The next best move, 45.g5, would drop White's winning chances from 92% to 9% (−6.18).","45.Ne7+ wins a rook, picking it up with 46.Nxc8.","Sets up a sequence and wins a rook once the captures settle, picking it up with 46.Nxc8.","Black answers 45…Kh7, leaving White with an isolated pawn on e7.","After 45…Kh7 46.Nxc8 b3 47.e7 b2 48.e8=Q, the pawn promotes on e8 and White comes out 13 points of material up (+5.92)."]},{"ply":94,"name":"47…Ke8","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"47…Ke8 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 3% to 0%. It allows mate in 2.","points":["White answers 48.Ke6.","Better was 47…Kf7 (+9.85): the king steps out of check to f7."],"better":"47…Kf7 prepares …b2 and …Kf6 and steps the king out of check to f7. After 48.Kd6 b2 49.Kd7 Kf6 50.e8=Q Kg5, the pawn promotes on e8 and Black is 8 points of material down (+9.85)."}],"opening":{"name":"Queen's Gambit Declined: Queen's Knight Variation","eco":"D31","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 7.Qb3: 13 moves in all, 7 by White and 6 by Black.","Theory runs out after 7.Qb3: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}